https://github.com/vim/vim/issues/11063
https://www.mail-archive.com/vim_dev@googlegroups.com/msg63877.html

Patch 9.0.0394 changed channel_read() to keep reading while the file
descriptor is ready, instead of stopping after a short read.  That avoids
treating a short read as if it meant that all available data had been drained.

On Solaris, test_terminal2.vim's Test_term_getcursor() can read terminal data
and then get EOF from the same file descriptor in the same channel_read()
invocation.  That EOF is reported by read() returning zero after the queued
data has been read.  The data is queued, but the EOF is ignored because
channel_read() only closes the channel part when no data was read at all.

Linux ptys behaved differently in the same tests: after reading queued data,
the closed slave side was reported as read() returning -1 with errno set to
EIO.  A later channel_read() invocation sees the same error with no data read,
so the existing readlen <= 0 check closes the channel part there.  Solaris can
complete the drain with read() returning zero in the same invocation, leaving
no later read event to trigger that existing close path.

Handle a read() result of zero as EOF even if bytes were read earlier in the
same channel_read() call.  This keeps the 9.0.0394 short-read behavior while
still closing the channel part when the peer has closed after the queued data.


--- vim-9.2.0670/src/channel.c
+++ vim-9.2.0670/src/channel.c
@@ -4332,7 +4332,7 @@
     }
 
     // Reading a disconnection (readlen == 0), or an error.
-    if (readlen <= 0)
+    if (len == 0 || readlen <= 0)
     {
 	if (!channel->ch_keep_open)
 	    ch_close_part_on_error(channel, part, (len < 0), func);
